if the CW's crisis on Infinite Earths  crossover left you a little confused  don't worry you're not alone the good  news is that we're here to sort out a  few of its more convoluted aspects and  explain just what's going on and why  who exactly is the guy who shows up in  purgatory and bestows seemingly  unfathomable cosmic powers on Oliver  Queen he identifies himself as Jim  Corrigan the spectre but if you haven't  had your nose buried in DC Comics for  the better part of your life that  probably didn't answer any questions  despite being a relatively obscure  character The Spectre is a favorite for  this kind of world shattering event  because he's the literal embodiment of  the wrath of God he's less of a person  and more of a force of nature a vessel  for divine fury with cosmic power even  Superman could only imagine  unfortunately it turned out that turning  God's vengeance into a person without  any mitigating force could lead to some  abuses of power to temper his vengeance  and create a new superhero in 1940 the  presents found the specter to Jim  Corrigan a cop who had been killed by  criminals returning him to life as a  ghostly superhero eventually Corrigan  worked off enough of his sins that he  was allowed to move on to his final  reward and the spectre force was passed  down to others after his death Green  Lantern Hal Jordan was the spectre for a  while and then Gotham City detective  Chris Allen was drafted into the job  it's never been handed off to Oliver  Queen in the comics but the fact that  both characters wear costumes built  around green hoods makes the shift in  wardrobe and an easy one most heroes  only get one chance to sacrifice their  lives to save the world  Oliver Queen got to once as Green Arrow  fighting to the death against the anti  monitors army of Shadows and then again  as the spectre seemingly sacrificing his  life to end the threat to the multiverse  the ante monitors death didn't really  stick though so is Oliver really dead  I told you dying is easy part the answer  most likely is a frustrating one yes and  no Oliver is definitely dead  he's the specter and as the name implies  the specter is quite literally a ghost  that said as far as superheroes are  concerned death is not a permanent state  the real question is whether Oliver  Queen will stick around in his new  phantasmal form and the answer to that  is probably if he's the spectre that  means he's beyond the traditional  boundaries of life and death  meaning that he has a pretty good chance  of coming back as the driving force of a  future CW crossover if Stephen Amell  ever gets bored with whatever he's going  to do after eight seasons of arrow with  a distinct possibility he'll jump into  professional wrestling one of the major  selling points for the CW crisis was  that it included nods to the entire  history of DC Comics films and TV shows  some like the Smallville scene or the  visit to earth 99 were pretty relevant  to the plot while others were basically  just cameos and they might have gone by  too fast for viewers who don't have vast  portions of who's who in the DC Universe  memorized to keep up with this is  another reference to the original crisis  on Infinite Earths comic which also  opens with the destruction of a handful  of DC's long-standing parallel worlds  complete with the red skies and fade to  white while the comic starts off by  obliterating earth 3 the show opens up  with two incarnations of the Dark Knight  being wiped out of existence earth 89  and earth 66 setting the trend of  Earth's mostly being numbered the years  that they debuted neither of the earths  the event opens with actually show that  world's version of its main character  bat men on earth 89 we catch up with the  intrepid reporter Alex NOx and Danny  Elfman's classic score letting us know  we're in the world of the 1989 Batman  film after that we skip over to the  decidedly retro earth 66 where Burt Ward  appears with a color scheme that lets us  know we're seeing the robbing of the  classic Batman TV series hey  we also see the Huntress running around  on rooftops on earth 203 a reference to  the birds of prey series which aired its  single season back in 2003 there are a  few notable exceptions to the year rule  though for instance we see a Superman  killed by Lex Luthor on earth 75 it's  actually a reference to a single comic  the shot of Superman and Lois that we  see on the news is lifted from 1994 s  Superman 75 the climax of the famous  death of Superman's story in which the  Man of Steel was killed by doomsday when  the Superman of Earth 96 joins up with  the crew there are a few jokes made  about how weird it is that he looks  exactly like Ray Palmer although no one  ever bothers to give a reason why the  obvious real-world reason is that both  characters are played by Brandon Ralph  who starred as the Man of Steel in 2006  as Superman Returns Superman 96 is  pretty clearly meant to be the same  Superman played by Christopher Reeve in  the 1978 Superman film and it's three  sequels and Ralph has played that  character before Superman Returns was  meant to pick up after Superman 2  ignoring the last two movies when he  sees Lois we get a brief excerpt of can  you read my mind the love theme from  Superman the movie and then there's this  line actually  this is the second time I've done  nothing for myself that's a callback to  Superman 3 where Superman splits into  Clark Kent and Superman to fight himself  in a junkyard  but why is he on earth 96 and not earth  206 or earth 78 Ralph's Man of Steel  isn't just from the movies it's also a  clear reference to Mark Waid in Alex  Ross's landmark 1996 comics miniseries  Kingdom Come the deaths of Lois Lane and  the rest of the Daily Planet staff at  the hands of the Joker are lifted from  that story as is the black and red  emblem that Superman wears in mourning  one of the grimmest moments of crisis on  Infinite Earths comes when Supergirl and  Batwoman take a visit to earth 99 Kevin  Conroy best known for his starring role  in Batman the Animated Series starting  in 1992 plays a version of Batman who is  given into his darkest impulses Bruce 99  appears to be in an incredible amount of  pain both mental and physical but we  don't get a lot of details as to what  happened to make him this way he does  mention that his world's version of Cate  Kane had been killed in action as that  woman helping push him over the edge  Luke Fox tells Supergirl that many of  his injuries are the result of his fight  with Superman though that doesn't seem  to be the entire truth of the matter  like Superman 96 Bruce 99 takes his look  from Kingdom Come where he's depicted  wearing a similar exoskeleton in their  annotations to that story Wayde and Ross  told readers that there was no single  event that caused this level of injury  and that it was just the toll of years  and years of getting in multiple  fistfights with Gotham's weirdest  villains the 99 by the way is probably a  reference to Batman Beyond which  premiered in 1999 and where Conroy  voiced in older Bruce who was a bit worn  down but not villainous in recent memory  Tom Welling has played Clark Kent longer  than anyone else starring in 10 seasons  of Smallville but he only once dawn  Superman's red and blues throughout the  show's run so it makes sense that when  the heroes of the arrowverse catch up  with Smallville's Clark on earth 167 he  still isn't Superman much to Lex  Luthor's disgust  Clark actually gave  his powers to focus on starting a family  with Lois you gave up your powers can't  say I miss these chats you are basically  a god you could fly this particular plot  point seems to be loosely inspired by  Alan Moore and Kurt swans whatever  happened to the man of tomorrow the  final Superman story before the end of  the original crisis on Infinite Earths  comics in that story it's revealed that  Superman gave up his powers by exposing  himself to gold kryptonite which  permanently strips Kryptonians of their  metahuman abilities it's pretty safe to  assume that Smallville's Clark did the  same especially since gold kryptonite  was introduced in Smallville's last  season the comic book crisis on Infinite  Earths streamlines DC's comic book  multiverse which had spread out over  worlds including earth to earth 3 Earth  X Earth C earth C minus and so on  into one single universe the TV version  has done the same putting the main CW  shows into one cohesive earth Prime one  of the reasons that DC had such a  convoluted multiverse is that unlike  their competition at Marvel where the  universe and many of its core characters  had been created by a relatively small  group of people and focused mainly on  New York the DC universe was much more  of a patchwork the characters had mostly  been created in isolation  sometimes by different publishers who  were acquired by DC after the fact the  same goes for the show's arrow came  first and spun off directly into flash  legends of tomorrow and Batwoman  but Supergirl originally aired on CBS  and was completely separate from the CW  shows until a crossover established it  as a parallel world Black Lightning  started off on the CW but it was set in  a different universe in hopes of  establishing its lesser-known hero  outside of the shadow of the other  characters with Supergirl flash Batwoman  legends of tomorrow Black Lightning and  arrow now combined into the newly  christened earth prime there's a shared  history creating a world where all the  characters exist alongside each other so  after eight years of arrow  we finally have a world where Superman  and Batman coexist with the other heroes  finally making the DC TV universe feel  whole characters can interact without  the need for another world  during interdimensional crossover I mean  how often does the world almost come to  an end almost like that so it's likely  that we could see more instances of the  shows crossing over in the future it's a  lot easier to have the flash hang out  with Supergirl Winn he doesn't have to  vibrate to a parallel world the DC TV  multiverse has been at the center of  several crossovers that have become an  annual tradition a major character on  the flash Harrison wells is built  entirely around swapping out with his  own interdimensional counterparts every  now and then forcing for Tom Cavanagh to  do a new accent every couple of months  while ostensibly playing the same guy  with that mind it seems like the show's  would be giving up a lot of potential  stories with everything combined into  the new earth Prime  but unlike the comic the show's didn't  actually ditch the idea of the  multiverse the main CW shows have all  been added to a single earth with the in  story reason being that they're the home  worlds of the seven Paragon characters  who saved the entirety of existence but  there are plenty of other worlds out  there in addition to confirming that the  DC films are a part of this multiverse  through a surprise cameo from movie  flash Ezra Miller it also preserves some  of the other shows the show streaming on  DC Universe make it through with Earth's  9 19 and 21 being established as the  settings for Titans Swamp Thing and Doom  Patrol respectively earth 96 is shown to  survive with Brandon Roth's older  Superman and we also see earth 12 hinted  at being the setting for HBO max's  upcoming Green Lantern show for longtime  comic book readers the most interesting  reveal might be the all-new earth 2  which will be the home of the upcoming  star girl show in the comics earth 2 was  the home of the original Golden Age  heroes of the Justice Society of America  who debuted in the 40s and as time went  on their descendants star girl herself  was a reimagining of one of those earth  two characters the star-spangled kid and  with the show drawing on the histories  of JSA characters such as Wildcat and  dr. midnight setting it there makes a  lot of sense it allows her world  have a different history playing into  the idea of legacy that's so core to her  character without interfering with the  new history of earth Prime while the  original comics crisis is largely known  as the crossover that got rid of earth  to the TV version is one that has  introduced it to a whole new generation  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite superhero shows are coming  soon subscribe to our YouTube channel  and hit the belt so you don't miss a  single one  [Music]
 

